Note : A square matrix M is invertible if det(M) or | M | $$\ne$$ 0.

Let $$M = \left[ {\matrix{
   a & b  \cr 
   b & c  \cr 

 } } \right]$$

(a) Given that $$\left[ {\matrix{
   a  \cr 
   b  \cr 

 } } \right] = \left[ {\matrix{
   b  \cr 
   c  \cr 

 } } \right]$$

$$ \Rightarrow a = b = c = \alpha $$ (let)

$$ \Rightarrow M = \left[ {\matrix{
   \alpha  & \alpha   \cr 
   \alpha  & \alpha   \cr 

 } } \right]$$

$$\Rightarrow$$ | M | = 0

$$\Rightarrow$$ M is non-invertible.

(b) Given that [bc] = [ab]

$$ \Rightarrow a = b = c = \alpha $$ (let)

Again | M | = 0

$$\Rightarrow$$ M is non-invertible.

(c) As given $$M = \left[ {\matrix{
   a & 0  \cr 
   0 & c  \cr 

 } } \right]$$

$$ \Rightarrow \left| M \right| = ac \ne 0$$ ($$\because$$ a and c are non-zero)

$$\Rightarrow$$ M is invertible.

(d) $$M = \left[ {\matrix{
   a & b  \cr 
   b & c  \cr 

 } } \right] \Rightarrow \left| M \right| = ac - {b^2} \ne 0$$

$$\because$$ ac is not equal to square of an integer.

$$\therefore$$ M is invertible.

